# 7. IMPORTANT COMMANDS AND THEIR PURPOSES

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| docker --version | Checks the Docker version. |
| minikube version | Checks the Minikube version. |
| kubectl version --client | Checks the kubectl client version. |
| docker build -t ai-model-app:latest . | Builds the Docker image. |
| minikube start --driver=docker | Starts the local Kubernetes cluster. |
| kubectl get nodes | Displays Kubernetes nodes. |
| minikube image load ai-model-app:latest | Loads the local Docker image into Minikube. |
| kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml | Creates or updates the Deployment. |
| kubectl get deployments | Displays Deployment status. |
| kubectl get pods | Displays application pods. |
| kubectl scale deployment ai-model-app --replicas=3 | Scales the application to three replicas. |
| kubectl scale deployment ai-model-app --replicas=1 | Scales the application back to one replica. |
| kubectl port-forward deployment/ai-model-app 5000:5000 | Forwards a local port to the application. |
| kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml | Deletes the Deployment. |
| minikube stop | Stops the Minikube cluster. |

# 8. COMMON ERRORS AND SOLUTIONS

**Error 1: Docker is not recognized**

Check whether Docker Desktop is installed. If it is installed, reopen the terminal and check again.

**Error 2: Docker cannot connect**

Open Docker Desktop and wait until it is running.

**Error 3: Minikube is not recognized**

Install Minikube and reopen the terminal.

**Error 4: kubectl is not recognized**

Install kubectl and reopen the terminal.

**Error 5: The project folder cannot be found**

Check the actual folder location and use the correct path in the cd command.

**Error 6: The pod shows ImagePullBackOff**

Check the image name in deployment.yaml and run:

```powershell
minikube image load ai-model-app:latest
```

**Error 7: The pod shows CrashLoopBackOff**

Check the application logs:

```powershell
kubectl logs deployment/ai-model-app
```

Read the error message to identify the problem.

**Error 8: The browser cannot open the application**

Make sure the pod is ready and the port-forward command is still running.

**Error 9: Three pods do not appear immediately**

Wait briefly and run:

```powershell
kubectl get pods
```

Kubernetes may need time to create and start the additional pods.

# 9. VIVA QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS

**Q1. What is Docker?**

Docker packages applications and their required files into images.

**Q2. What is a Docker image?**

A Docker image is a package used to create containers.

**Q3. What is a container?**

A container is an isolated environment in which an application runs.

**Q4. What is Kubernetes?**

Kubernetes is a system used to manage containerized applications.

**Q5. What is Minikube?**

Minikube runs a small Kubernetes cluster on a local computer.

**Q6. What is kubectl?**

kubectl is a command-line tool used to communicate with Kubernetes.

**Q7. What is a pod?**

A pod is the smallest unit Kubernetes manages. It contains one or more containers.

**Q8. What is a Deployment?**

A Deployment manages application pods and helps maintain the requested number of replicas.

**Q9. What is scaling?**

Scaling means increasing or decreasing the number of application replicas.

**Q10. What does replicas: 3 mean?**

It tells Kubernetes to maintain three copies of the application.

**Q11. What does kubectl get pods do?**

It displays the pods and their status.

**Q12. What does kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml do?**

It creates or updates Kubernetes resources using the YAML file.

**Q13. Why do we use minikube image load?**

It makes the locally built Docker image available inside Minikube.

**Q14. Why do we use port-forwarding?**

It allows us to access the application through a local port.

**Q15. What is the purpose of a Dockerfile?**

A Dockerfile contains instructions for building a Docker image.

**Q16. Is this a real AI model?**

No. This application is a demonstration used to learn Docker, Kubernetes, deployment, and scaling.

# 10. RESULT

A local Kubernetes cluster was created using Minikube. The Dockerized application was deployed successfully, accessed through a web browser, and scaled from one replica to three replicas. The application was then scaled back to one replica.

The practical demonstrates how Docker and Kubernetes can be used to deploy and scale an application on a local computer.
